The Core Idea

Most inference optimisation assumes abundant high-end hardware. I took the opposite path: I built under real, painful constraints (limited RAM, thermals, and a single consumer mini-PC). Cerberus aggressively owns its own memory through a tiered architecture and tries to keep all accelerators — especially the NPU — meaningfully utilised even when resources are tight.
I’m particularly interested in whether some of these constraint-driven techniques could help unlock better utilisation of underutilised NPUs that exist on many enterprise and workstation platforms but often remain largely idle compared to the GPUs.
I have no illusions about scale. I’m not claiming this competes with clustered high-end systems. I’m exploring whether ideas born from having no choice but to be efficient might still be useful when you do have better hardware — particularly around making the NPU a first-class citizen rather than an afterthought.

